CyberArk IDIRA + Salesforce
Privileged SAML SSO with Passkey / Phishing-Resistant MFA
Proof of Concept Implementation Runbook

Document Version	1.0
Date	September 21, 2026
Scope	CyberArk Identity / IDIRA as SAML IdP; Salesforce as SAML SP
Primary Test User	Jaime (existing test account; elevated during POC)
Purpose	Validate phishing-resistant passkey authentication and Salesforce recognition of SAML AMR/ACR signals

POC safety principle: Keep a tested non-SSO Salesforce administrator and the Salesforce Login Form available throughout the POC. Do not make SAML the only login path until testing and rollback have been proven.

 
1. Purpose and POC Outcome
This runbook implements and validates a controlled proof of concept in which CyberArk Identity/IDIRA authenticates a user with a phishing-resistant passkey and sends a signed SAML response to Salesforce. Salesforce operates as the SAML Service Provider (SP) and must recognize the authentication as phishing-resistant from an accepted AMR or ACR signal before granting a privileged Salesforce session without an additional Salesforce-hosted passkey challenge.
Architecture correction: CyberArk Identity/IDIRA is the SAML Identity Provider (IdP). Salesforce is the SAML Service Provider (SP).

1.1 POC success criteria
•	Jaime can launch the Salesforce POC application from the CyberArk User Portal and is challenged with the configured CyberArk passkey/FIDO2 method.
•	CyberArk sends a signed SAML response whose Issuer, Audience/Entity ID, Recipient/ACS URL, NameID/user identifier, and signature validate successfully in Salesforce.
•	The SAML response contains the already-configured AMR and/or ACR signal and Salesforce classifies at least one signal as Phishing-Resistant MFA.
•	After Jaime is elevated to a Salesforce privileged/admin scope, the SSO login still succeeds without Salesforce requiring a second Salesforce-native passkey enrollment/challenge.
•	Salesforce Login History records the expected Authentication Method Reference and/or Authentication Context Class Reference for the SSO login.
•	A second Salesforce test user in the same Salesforce org can authenticate through the same Salesforce SAML SP configuration and same CyberArk Web-SAML app using a different NameID/Federation ID. This proves that a separate SAML SP configuration is not required merely because the Salesforce user is different.
•	An unauthorized CyberArk user cannot run the Salesforce POC application.
•	Rollback is verified and the pre-POC direct Salesforce administrative login remains usable.
1.2 Key design rule: one Salesforce org, multiple users
For this POC, treat the Salesforce organization/My Domain as the SAML service provider. A single SAML SSO configuration can authenticate multiple Salesforce users in that same org. User selection is performed by the identity carried in the SAML assertion (for example, NameID mapped to Federation ID or Salesforce username). Create a separate CyberArk SAML application when there is a separate Salesforce org/SP configuration, not simply because there is another Salesforce user.
Shared-account caution: If multiple human CyberArk identities are deliberately mapped to one shared Salesforce administrator account, the test can prove SAML authentication but does not provide native per-human Salesforce accountability. For the cleanest POC of “one SP configuration supports multiple users,” use two separate Salesforce test users with unique identifiers.

2. Target Architecture and Authentication Flow
1. User	2. CyberArk Identity / IDIRA	3. Passkey Policy	4. SAML Response	5. Salesforce SP
Jaime / Test User	Identity Role grants app access	FIDO2 / passkey step-up	Signed assertion + NameID + AMR/ACR	Validate assertion, map local user, evaluate PR-MFA

1.	User authenticates to the CyberArk User Portal.
2.	CyberArk role membership makes the Salesforce POC tile available.
3.	User launches the Salesforce POC tile.
4.	The Web App Policy invokes the dedicated passkey authentication profile.
5.	CyberArk validates the enrolled passkey/FIDO2 authenticator.
6.	CyberArk issues a signed SAMLResponse to the Salesforce ACS endpoint.
7.	Salesforce validates the signature, issuer, audience, recipient, time conditions, and user identifier.
8.	Salesforce evaluates AMR/ACR. A recognized phishing-resistant signal (for example, fido2 or passkey) satisfies the privileged-user PR-MFA requirement.
9.	Salesforce creates the session for the mapped local Salesforce account.
10.	Validation evidence is collected from CyberArk activity/audit records, the SAML response, Salesforce SAML Assertion Validator, and Salesforce Login History.
3. Scope, Assumptions, and Safeguards
•	The POC is performed in a Salesforce test/sandbox org or another explicitly approved non-production org.
•	The Salesforce My Domain is already deployed.
•	Jaime already exists in Salesforce as a local test user and in CyberArk Identity as a test user.
•	The test identity already has an enrolled CyberArk passkey/FIDO2 authenticator, or passkey enrollment is available and has been proven.
•	The existing CyberArk test configuration already generates AMR and/or ACR signals. This runbook treats those signals as an existing prerequisite and focuses on preserving and validating them in the Salesforce SAML assertion.
•	The POC uses a dedicated CyberArk role, authentication profile, and Web-SAML application so the test can be removed cleanly.
•	A separate Salesforce administrator account remains available for direct login and is not dependent on the POC SAML configuration.
•	The Salesforce Login Form remains selected during the POC so direct login and SSO can coexist.
•	All timestamps on participating systems are accurate enough for SAML NotBefore/NotOnOrAfter validation.
