4. Phase A - CyberArk Identity / IDIRA Preparation
4.1 Create the dedicated POC role
A1. Open Identity Administration and go to Core Services > Roles.
A2. Select Add Role and create `SF-CLMAdmin-POC-Users`. — Use a description that clearly states this is a temporary Salesforce SAML/passkey POC role.
A3. Open Members > Add and add Jaime. — Add a second test identity now if you intend to execute the one-SP/multiple-user validation.
A4. Save the role and record its membership before testing.
Why this matters: CyberArk roles are the entitlement boundary for the POC app. The Salesforce SAML app should be deployed to this role rather than to broad tenant roles.

4.2 Verify passkey enrollment and existing PR-MFA signaling
A5. Confirm that Jaime has a CyberArk passkey/FIDO2 authenticator enrolled. — The POC should use the same method already proven during the CyberArk working session (for example, an on-device authenticator such as Windows Hello where that is your approved method).
A6. Confirm the test account can successfully complete a CyberArk passkey challenge before integrating Salesforce.
A7. Record the currently proven AMR/ACR behavior. — Capture the exact value(s) that the existing test configuration emits. Salesforce accepts phishing-resistant values including `fido2` and `passkey`; use the exact already-tested value rather than introducing a new value during the POC.
Do not weaken the test: Do not replace the known passkey flow with SMS, email OTP, TOTP, or another standard-strength factor for the privileged-user validation. The objective is to prove phishing-resistant authentication end to end.

4.3 Create a dedicated passkey authentication profile
A8. Go to Settings > Authentication > Authentication Profiles and select Add Profile.
A9. Name the profile `SF-PR-MFA-Passkey-POC`.
A10. Configure the profile to require the tenant's approved FIDO2/passkey mechanism used in the existing successful test. — Do not include weaker fallback mechanisms in this dedicated POC profile unless CyberArk specifically requires them for bootstrap/recovery and they are excluded from the successful test path.
A11. Set the challenge pass-through behavior so the POC can reliably demonstrate a fresh passkey event. — If the tenant reuses an existing MFA session, use a private browser/new CyberArk session between tests and document the behavior rather than assuming each tile click always produces a new challenge.
A12. Save the profile.
4.4 Create the CyberArk Web-SAML application shell
A13. Go to Apps & Widgets > Web Apps > Add Web Apps.
A14. Open the Custom tab, add the SAML template, confirm the addition, and close the catalog.
A15. On Settings, name the application `Salesforce - CLMAdmin POC`. — Add a description identifying the Salesforce test org and POC owner.
A16. Open Trust > Identity Provider Configuration and export/download the CyberArk IdP metadata. — This metadata is used to create the Salesforce SAML SSO setting and provides the IdP issuer, sign-on endpoint, and signing certificate information.
A17. Save the CyberArk app shell. — Do not deploy it to end users yet; the Salesforce SP values are populated in Phase C.
5. Phase B - Salesforce Service Provider Configuration
5.1 Preserve an independent login path
B1. Sign in to Salesforce with a non-POC administrator account and verify direct login works.
B2. Go to Setup > My Domain > Authentication Configuration > Edit and confirm Login Form is selected. — Leave it selected for the full POC. This preserves direct Salesforce login alongside SSO.
B3. Record the pre-POC My Domain authentication settings and existing SAML SSO records.
Break-glass rule: Do not use Jaime as the only administrator capable of reverting the SSO configuration.

5.2 Create the SAML SSO setting in Salesforce
B4. Go to Setup > Single Sign-On Settings.
B5. If SAML is not already enabled, select Edit, enable SAML, and save.
B6. Create a new POC SAML configuration. — Prefer New from Metadata File using the CyberArk IdP metadata exported in A16. If metadata import is not used, create the setting manually using the exact CyberArk IdP values.
B7. Name the SSO setting `CyberArk-IDIRA-POC`.
B8. Verify the Issuer exactly matches the CyberArk IdP issuer in the SAML assertion/metadata.
B9. Verify the Identity Provider Certificate is the CyberArk signing certificate from the IdP metadata.
B10. Set the Salesforce Entity ID to the Salesforce SP identifier for this test org. — For most org SSO configurations Salesforce documents a URL identifying the Salesforce instance/My Domain. The value saved here must match an Audience value sent by CyberArk.
B11. Choose the SAML Identity Type and Identity Location that match the POC assertion design. — Recommended for this POC: use an existing unique Federation ID if that is the established mapping, and place the identifier in the NameID/Subject. If the org is already standardized on Salesforce username, keep that mapping instead. Do not mix identity types between Salesforce and CyberArk.
B12. Configure the Service Provider Initiated Request Binding and Identity Provider Login URL if SP-initiated testing is in scope. — Use the binding and IdP URL supported by the CyberArk configuration. IdP-initiated testing from the CyberArk tile does not require an AuthnRequest from Salesforce.
B13. Save the SAML SSO setting.
5.3 Capture Salesforce SP values
B14. Open the saved `CyberArk-IDIRA-POC` SSO record and record the Salesforce Entity ID.
B15. Record the Salesforce SAML Login URL / ACS endpoint generated for the org.
B16. Download the Salesforce metadata and retain it with the POC evidence. — Use it as the authoritative source for the Entity ID and endpoints that will be entered into CyberArk.
5.4 Map the Jaime Salesforce test user
B17. Go to Setup > Users > Users and open Jaime.
B18. Record Jaime's current profile, permission sets, Federation ID, and username before changes.
B19. Populate or verify the identifier used by the SAML mapping. — If the POC uses Federation ID, its value must exactly match the identifier CyberArk will place in the assertion for Jaime. Keep uniqueness and case handling consistent with the org setting.
B20. Do not elevate Jaime yet. — First complete one baseline SAML login as the current test user. Privilege elevation is performed after the base SAML trust is proven, which makes troubleshooting much cleaner.
6. Phase C - Complete the CyberArk Salesforce SAML Application
6.1 Configure Salesforce as the Service Provider in CyberArk
C1. Return to Apps & Widgets > Web Apps > `Salesforce - CLMAdmin POC` > Trust.
C2. Configure the Service Provider section from Salesforce metadata where possible. — If configuring manually, enter the Salesforce Entity ID as SP Entity ID / Issuer / Audience and the Salesforce SAML Login URL as the ACS URL.
C3. Verify Recipient handling matches the Salesforce ACS requirement. — If the CyberArk option is “Recipient same as ACS URL,” use it only when the Salesforce metadata/configuration indicates the same endpoint.
C4. Verify the signing method and signing certificate. — The certificate presented in the CyberArk IdP metadata must be the certificate configured on the Salesforce SSO record. Record the certificate expiration date.
6.2 Configure the SAML user identity
C5. Open the SAML Response section of the CyberArk application.
C6. Set NameID to the CyberArk user attribute whose value equals Jaime's Salesforce mapping identifier. — For a Federation ID design, the resulting NameID value must equal Jaime's Salesforce Federation ID. For a Salesforce username design, it must equal the Salesforce username.
C7. Set NameID Format to the value expected by the Salesforce SSO setting/metadata. — Do not change the format solely to make the string look like an email address; the Salesforce identity-location/type settings and CyberArk assertion must agree.
6.3 Preserve and validate AMR/ACR signaling
C8. Retain the existing CyberArk configuration that already emits the proven AMR/ACR signal for the test account.
C9. For AMR, verify the outbound SAML attribute name contains `amr` or `authnmethodsreferences` and the value resolves to a Salesforce-recognized phishing-resistant token. — Salesforce currently recognizes values such as `fido2`, `passkey`, `phr`, `hwk`, `pki`, and others. If multiple AMR values are sent in a string, Salesforce documents semicolon-separated values; do not substitute comma-separated values.
C10. For ACR, verify the AuthnContextClassRef contains a Salesforce-recognized phishing-resistant token.
C11. Verify the signal is tied to the actual passkey-authenticated transaction. — Do not create a static “fido2” claim that would be sent even when a different authentication method was used.
Salesforce evaluation rule: Salesforce requires at least one accepted AMR or ACR signal that meets the user’s required tier. For a privileged user, the signal must classify as Phishing-Resistant MFA. If several signals are present, Salesforce uses the strongest recognized value.

6.4 Assign the application and enforce passkey step-up
C12. Open Permissions on the CyberArk Web-SAML app and add `SF-CLMAdmin-POC-Users`.
C13. Grant the role the normal end-user app permissions required to see and run the application. — For a standard CyberArk deployed web app this typically includes View, Run, and Automatically Deploy; verify against your tenant’s permission model.
C14. Open the application Policy tab.
C15. Set the application Default Profile to `SF-PR-MFA-Passkey-POC`. — This places the passkey step-up directly in front of the Salesforce POC app. If your tenant uses an existing Web App Access Orchestration for this use case, select that orchestration only if it invokes the same dedicated passkey profile.
C16. Save the Web-SAML app and confirm its status is deployed to the POC role.
7. Phase D - Enable Salesforce SSO for the POC
D1. Return to Salesforce Setup > My Domain > Authentication Configuration > Edit.
D2. Select the new `CyberArk-IDIRA-POC` authentication service.
D3. Keep Login Form selected.
D4. Save and open the Salesforce My Domain login page in a private browser. — Confirm both the Salesforce login form and the CyberArk SSO option are available.
D5. Do not remove or disable any existing production authentication service as part of this POC.
8. Phase E - Baseline Login, Privilege Elevation, and Final Test
8.1 Baseline SAML login before privilege elevation
E1. Open a fresh private/incognito browser session and sign in to the CyberArk User Portal as Jaime.
E2. Verify the `Salesforce - CLMAdmin POC` tile is visible.
E3. Launch the tile and complete the CyberArk passkey challenge.
E4. Confirm Salesforce logs Jaime into the intended local Salesforce user.
E5. Capture the SAML response and validate it in Salesforce Setup > Single Sign-On Settings > SAML Assertion Validator.
E6. Confirm Issuer, Audience, Recipient/ACS, signature, NameID, and time conditions all validate successfully.
E7. Confirm the validator identifies at least one AMR/ACR value as phishing-resistant.
E8. Go to Salesforce Setup > Login History and verify the login row records the expected Authentication Context Class Reference and/or Authentication Method Reference.
8.2 Elevate Jaime into Salesforce privileged scope
E9. Using the independent Salesforce admin, elevate Jaime to the agreed administrative/privileged test scope. — For a clear POC, assigning the System Administrator profile is the simplest privileged condition if approved. Salesforce also treats users with permissions such as Modify All Data, View All Data, Customize Application, or Author Apex as privileged for PR-MFA enforcement.
E10. Record the exact elevation performed and the previous state for rollback.
E11. Sign Jaime out of Salesforce and CyberArk and close the test browser session.
8.3 Re-test as privileged Jaime
E12. Start a new private/incognito browser session and sign in to the CyberArk User Portal as Jaime.
E13. Launch `Salesforce - CLMAdmin POC` and complete the passkey challenge.
E14. Confirm Salesforce grants the session for privileged Jaime without forcing a Salesforce-native passkey prompt.
E15. Immediately inspect Salesforce Login History and the SAML Assertion Validator again. — Do not treat “no prompt” alone as evidence. The authoritative proof is the accepted AMR/ACR signal and successful assertion validation.
E16. Capture a screenshot/export of the Login History fields and the validator’s authentication-strength result for the POC evidence package.
9. Phase F - Prove One Salesforce SP Configuration Supports Multiple Users
This phase directly validates the architecture question raised during the design discussion: multiple Salesforce users in the same Salesforce organization do not inherently require separate SAML SP configurations.
F1. Use or create a second Salesforce test user in the same Salesforce org. — Use a separate named test user, not the same Jaime Salesforce account.
F2. Populate that user's Federation ID (or other configured identity field) with a unique value.
F3. Ensure the corresponding CyberArk test identity resolves to that unique value in NameID.
F4. Add the CyberArk identity to `SF-CLMAdmin-POC-Users`.
F5. Do not create another Salesforce SAML SSO record and do not clone the CyberArk Web-SAML app.
F6. From a fresh session, launch the same `Salesforce - CLMAdmin POC` tile as the second user.
F7. Confirm Salesforce maps the assertion to the second Salesforce local user while using the same Salesforce Entity ID, ACS endpoint, and CyberArk signing trust.
F8. Capture the second assertion and Login History entry as evidence.
Expected result: Both users authenticate through the same Salesforce SP configuration; their different NameID/Federation ID values determine which local Salesforce user is logged in.

10. POC Test Matrix and Evidence
Test	Procedure	Expected Result	Evidence	Pass/Fail
T01	CyberArk role entitlement	Jaime sees/runs only the authorized POC app	CyberArk app permissions / user portal	
T02	Passkey step-up	CyberArk invokes approved FIDO2/passkey profile	CyberArk authentication/audit event	
T03	SAML trust	Signature, issuer, audience, recipient, time conditions valid	Salesforce SAML Assertion Validator	
T04	Identity mapping	Jaime maps to correct Salesforce local user	NameID + Salesforce user/session	
T05	PR-MFA signal	AMR/ACR classified as phishing-resistant	Validator + Login History	
T06	Privileged Jaime	Admin/privileged login succeeds without Salesforce-native passkey prompt	Login History + screen evidence	
T07	One SP / multiple users	Second user maps through same SAML config	Second assertion + Login History	
T08	Unauthorized user	User outside POC role cannot run/receive app	CyberArk portal/permission evidence	
T09	Direct-login fallback	Independent Salesforce admin still logs in with Login Form	Private-browser direct login test	
T10	Optional SP-initiated SSO	My Domain SSO button redirects to CyberArk and returns to Salesforce	Browser trace + Login History	
10.1 Evidence package
•	CyberArk POC role membership screenshot/export.
•	CyberArk passkey authentication profile configuration.
•	CyberArk Web-SAML Trust page values, with secrets/private keys excluded.
•	CyberArk SAML Response mapping showing the user identifier and AMR/ACR mechanism.
•	Sanitized raw SAML assertion for baseline and privileged Jaime tests.
•	Salesforce SAML Assertion Validator results showing authentication strength.
•	Salesforce Login History with Authentication Context Class Reference and Authentication Method Reference columns.
•	Jaime before/after Salesforce profile/permission record.
•	Second-user assertion demonstrating same Entity ID/ACS with different user identifier.
•	Rollback confirmation.
11. Troubleshooting Decision Guide
Symptom	Most likely validation point	Action
Salesforce says user not found	NameID / identity type / identity location	Compare the assertion identifier byte-for-byte with Jaime’s Federation ID or username and verify Salesforce SAML Identity Type/Location.
Audience validation fails	Salesforce Entity ID vs CyberArk Audience	Copy the Entity ID from the saved Salesforce SSO setting/metadata and use exactly that value as the CyberArk SP Entity ID/Audience.
Recipient or destination error	ACS URL mismatch	Use the Salesforce-generated SAML Login URL/ACS from the SSO setting or metadata.
Signature invalid	IdP certificate mismatch or assertion alteration	Verify the CyberArk signing certificate configured in Salesforce. Validate an unmodified raw assertion; copy/paste formatting can invalidate signatures.
Salesforce prompts privileged user to create passkey	AMR/ACR missing, weak, or unrecognized	Check Salesforce Login History and SAML Assertion Validator. Verify the signal is in Salesforce’s phishing-resistant tier and is sent in the accepted AMR/ACR format.
No CyberArk passkey prompt	Existing CyberArk session/pass-through or app policy not applied	Use a new private session and confirm the Web App Policy default profile is the dedicated passkey profile.
Wrong Salesforce user opens	NameID mapping collision/wrong attribute	Inspect NameID and confirm uniqueness of the Salesforce Federation ID/username mapping.
SP-initiated login loops	My Domain authentication service / IdP login URL / request binding	Keep IdP-initiated tile flow as the baseline; then verify My Domain Authentication Configuration and SP-initiated fields separately.
Second user requires a new SAML app	Identity mapping is hard-coded to Jaime	Replace any fixed account mapping with a per-user attribute that resolves to each Salesforce user’s unique identifier.
12. Rollback Plan
Rollback objective: Return both platforms to their pre-POC authentication state without affecting unrelated SSO applications or Salesforce users.

12.1 Salesforce rollback
SF-R1. Use the independent Salesforce administrator account.
SF-R2. In Setup > My Domain > Authentication Configuration, deselect `CyberArk-IDIRA-POC` as an authentication service while leaving Login Form enabled.
SF-R3. Restore Jaime’s original profile and/or remove the POC privilege elevation.
SF-R4. Restore Jaime’s original Federation ID only if it was changed solely for the POC and no other integration depends on it.
SF-R5. Leave the POC SAML SSO record disabled/unselected until evidence review is complete; delete it only after the team confirms the POC no longer needs to be reproduced.
SF-R6. Confirm direct login still works and record the post-rollback Login History.
12.2 CyberArk rollback
CA-R1. Remove `SF-CLMAdmin-POC-Users` from the Salesforce POC app permissions or undeploy the app.
CA-R2. Remove test users from the POC role.
CA-R3. Disable/delete the `Salesforce - CLMAdmin POC` Web-SAML application when no longer required.
CA-R4. Delete the `SF-PR-MFA-Passkey-POC` authentication profile only after confirming it is not referenced by any other application or policy.
CA-R5. Delete the POC role after verifying it has no remaining assignments.
CA-R6. Preserve sanitized logs/assertions and configuration evidence in the approved POC record location.
13. Production Readiness Items After a Successful POC
•	Decide whether the production design uses one CyberArk Web-SAML app per Salesforce org. Avoid per-user SAML apps unless a genuinely different SP configuration or post-login routing requirement exists.
•	Define the authoritative Salesforce identity mapping attribute and lifecycle process (Federation ID or other approved identifier).
•	Confirm AMR/ACR values are dynamically derived from the authentication actually performed and are not static application claims.
•	Define passkey enrollment, lost-device recovery, and break-glass procedures for privileged users.
•	Confirm certificate ownership, renewal alerting, and rollover procedure for the CyberArk SAML signing certificate.
•	Decide whether SP-initiated SSO is required in addition to CyberArk tile/IdP-initiated SSO.
•	Determine whether Salesforce SAML sessions must be classified as High Assurance for other Salesforce controls. Treat Salesforce session security level as a separate control from PR-MFA AMR/ACR evaluation.
•	Integrate production access with the approved CyberArk Identity Role/group lifecycle and access review process.
•	Preserve a non-SSO emergency administrator according to the organization’s break-glass policy.
•	If the business requirement maps multiple humans to a single Salesforce admin account, obtain Salesforce/security-governance approval separately; successful SAML authentication does not resolve the auditability implications of a shared application identity.
14. Vendor-Validated Facts Used in This Runbook
Topic	Validated point
CyberArk passkeys	CyberArk states that Identity supports phishing-resistant FIDO2 passkeys and that passkeys can be used for step-up authentication to web apps.
CyberArk SAML application capabilities	CyberArk’s published Application Management collection exposes Salesforce-relevant SAML fields including Audience, ACS URL, NameIDFormat, AuthnContextClass, SAML attributes, signing configuration, and SP metadata input.
CyberArk application entitlement	CyberArk web applications can be assigned to users/roles and the app Policy can invoke an authentication profile for step-up before application launch.
Salesforce SP configuration	Salesforce SAML SSO settings define the IdP issuer/certificate, Salesforce Entity ID, identity mapping, and SAML endpoints. The SAML Audience must match the Salesforce Entity ID.
Salesforce PR-MFA via SSO	For privileged users, Salesforce requires a phishing-resistant authentication signal from the SSO IdP. Salesforce evaluates SAML AMR and ACR signals and allows the user through without an additional Salesforce MFA prompt when an accepted signal is received.
Salesforce accepted PR signals	Salesforce currently lists phishing-resistant values including fido, fido2, passkey, phr, hwk, pki, smartcard, softwarepki, tlsclient, x509, and others, with slightly different AMR/ACR lists.
Salesforce evidence tools	Salesforce SAML Assertion Validator can evaluate SAML assertion errors and authentication strength; Login History can display Authentication Context Class Reference and Authentication Method Reference.
Salesforce My Domain safety	Salesforce My Domain Authentication Configuration can expose both the Login Form and configured SSO identity provider, allowing direct login and SSO to coexist during a POC.
